msgid "TLS registry reference"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The TLS Registry is a Quarkus extension that centralizes TLS configuration, "
"making it easier to manage and maintain secure connections across your "
"application."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When defining TLS configurations in a single centralized location, you can "
"use the TLS Registry to reference them from multiple components within the "
"application, ensuring consistency and reducing the risk of configuration "
"errors."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The TLS Registry consolidates settings and supports multiple named "
"configurations.\n"
"Therefore, you can tailor TLS settings for different application parts.\n"
"This flexibility is particularly useful when different components require "
"distinct security configurations."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The TLS Registry extension is automatically included in your project when "
"you use compatible extensions, such as Quarkus REST, gRPC, SmallRye GraphQL "
"Client\n"
", or Reactive Routes\n"
"."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As a result, applications that use the TLS Registry can be ready to handle "
"secure communications out of the box."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"TLS Registry also provides automatic certificate reloading\n"
", integration with Let's Encrypt (ACME)\n"
", support for Kubernetes Cert-Manager,\n"
"and compatibility with various keystore formats, such as PKCS12, PEM, and "
"JKS."
msgstr ""

msgid "Using the TLS registry"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To configure a TLS connection, including key and truststores, use the "
"`+quarkus.tls.*+` properties.\n"
"These properties are required for:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Setting up the default TLS configuration, defined directly under `+quarkus."
"tls.*+`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Creating separate, named configurations by using `+quarkus.tls.<name>.*+`.\n"
"By specifying the `+quarkus.tls.<name>.*+` properties, you can adapt the TLS "
"settings for a specific component."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The default TLS configuration is not a fallback or global configuration.\n"
"Each named TLS configuration, or \"TLS bucket,\" must provide its own "
"properties.\n"
"For instance, `quarkus.tls.reload-period` applies only to the default TLS "
"configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"As described in detail link:https://github.com/quarkusio/quarkus/blob/main/"
"adr/0004-using-the-tls-registry-for-clients.adoc#configuring-clients-with-"
"the-tls-registry[here], Quarkus client extensions (such as REST, GRPC, and "
"so on) ignore properties defined in default (that is, unnamed) TLS "
"configurations.\n"
"The `quarkus.tls.trust-all` property is the only exception."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring HTTPS for an HTTP server"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To ensure secure client-server communication, the client is often required "
"to verify the server's authenticity."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The server must use a keystore that contains its certificate and private key."
""
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The client needs to be configured with a truststore to validate the server's "
"certificate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"During the TLS handshake, the server presents its certificate, which the "
"client then validates.\n"
"This prevents man-in-the-middle attacks and secures data transmission."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following sections guide you through setting up HTTPS by using PEM or "
"PKCS12 keystore types.\n"
"In addition, they provide guidance on named configurations to specify and "
"manage multiple TLS configurations simultaneously, allowing you to define "
"distinct settings for each."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use one of the following configuration examples based on your keystore type:"
msgstr ""

msgid "*By using PEM files:*"
msgstr ""

msgid "*By using a `p12` (PKCS12) keystore:*"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Distinguishing multiple configurations with names:*"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring HTTPS for a client"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following example configures a gRPC client named \"hello\" to use HTTPS "
"with a truststore from the default TLS configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring mTLS"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To set up mutual TLS (mTLS) in your {project-name} application, configure "
"the server and the client by creating and managing both a keystore and a "
"truststore for each:"
msgstr ""

msgid "*Server keystore*: Contains the server's certificate and private key."
msgstr ""

msgid "*Client keystore*: Contains the client's certificate and private key."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Server truststore*: Stores the client's certificate for authenticating the "
"client."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Client truststore*: Stores the server's certificate for authenticating the "
"server."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This configuration enables mTLS by ensuring that both the server and the "
"client validate each other's certificates, which provides an additional "
"layer of security."
msgstr ""

msgid "Referencing a TLS configuration"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To reference an example _named_ configuration that you created by using the "
"`quarkus.tls.<name>.*` properties as explained in <<using-the-tls-"
"registry>>, use the `tls-configuration-name` property as shown in the "
"following examples:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When you configure the Typesafe GraphQL client with certificate reloading, "
"as described in the <<reloading-certificates>> section, override the bean "
"scope to `RequestScoped` or another scope shorter than the application scope."
"\n"
"The Typesafe client is an application-scoped bean by default."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A shorter scope ensures that instances created after a certificate reload "
"use the latest certificate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Dynamic clients are `@Dependent` scoped.\n"
"Inject dynamic clients into components with an appropriate scope."
msgstr ""

msgid "Referencing the default truststore of SunJSSE"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"JDK distributions typically contain a truststore in the `$JAVA_HOME/lib/"
"security/cacerts` file.\n"
"SunJSSE uses this truststore as the default truststore.\n"
"SunJSSE is the default implementation of the Java Secure Socket Extension "
"(JSSE)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Java runtime components, such as `javax.net.ssl.HttpsURLConnection`, rely on "
"JSSE for SSL and TLS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Although Quarkus extensions typically do not honor the default truststore of "
"SunJSSE, it is still practical to use it in some situations.\n"
"This applies when migrating from legacy technologies or running on a Linux "
"distribution where the SunJSSE truststore is synchronized with the operating "
"system (OS)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To simplify the use of the SunJSSE truststore, Quarkus TLS Registry provides "
"a TLS configuration under the name `javax.net.ssl` that mimics the default "
"behavior of SunJSSE:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If the `javax.net.ssl.trustStore` system property is defined, its value is "
"honored as a truststore."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Otherwise, the paths `$JAVA_HOME/lib/security/jssecacerts` and `$JAVA_HOME/"
"lib/security/cacerts` are checked, and the first existing file is used as a "
"truststore."
msgstr ""

msgid "If neither condition is met, an `IllegalStateException` is thrown."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The password to open the truststore is obtained from the `javax.net.ssl."
"trustStorePassword` system property.\n"
"If this property is not set, the default password `changeit` is used."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `javax.net.ssl` configuration can be used as a value for various `*.tls-"
"configuration-name` properties, as shown below:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `javax.net.ssl` TLS configuration can not be customized or overridden."
msgstr ""

msgid "Configuring TLS"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"TLS configuration primarily involves managing keystores and truststores.\n"
"The specific setup depends on the format used, such as PEM, P12, or JKS."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The following sections outline the available TLS configuration properties."
msgstr ""

msgid "Key stores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Key stores store private keys and certificates.\n"
"They are mainly used on the server side, but can also be used on the client "
"side when mTLS is used."
msgstr ""

msgid "PEM keystores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Privacy Enhanced Mail (PEM) keystores are composed of a list of file pairs:"
msgstr ""

msgid "*The certificate file* - a `.crt` or `.pem` file."
msgstr ""

msgid "*The private key file* - often a `.key` file."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a PEM keystore:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"In most cases, you only need a single pair consisting of a certificate and a "
"private key.\n"
"Even if the certificate is part of a certificate chain, it includes only one "
"private key that corresponds to the end-entity certificate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When multiple pairs are configured, the selection of one of the configured "
"pairs of certificates and private keys is based on Server Name Indication "
"(SNI).\n"
"The client sends the name of the server to which the client is attempting to "
"connect, and the server selects the appropriate pair of certificates and "
"private keys.\n"
"To use this feature, ensure that xref:./tls-registry-reference.adoc#sni[SNI] "
"is enabled on both the client and server."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When configuring multiple key pairs or certificate pairs, the server "
"executes the configured pairs in a lexicographical order of their names by "
"default, as demonstrated with `store.pem.a` and `store.pem.b` in the "
"previous example.\n"
"The pair with the lowest lexicographical order is executed first.\n"
"To change this, you can define the order by using the `quarkus.tls.key-store."
"pem.order` property.\n"
"For example, `quarkus.tls.key-store.pem.order=b,c,a`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This setting is important when using SNI, because it uses the first "
"specified pair as the default."
msgstr ""

msgid "When using a PEM keystore, the following formats are supported:"
msgstr ""

msgid "PKCS#8 private key (unencrypted)"
msgstr ""

msgid "PKCS#1 RSA private key (unencrypted)"
msgstr ""

msgid "Encrypted PKCS#8 private key (encrypted with AES-128-CBC)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For an encrypted PKCS#8 private key, set the `quarkus.tls.key-store.pem."
"password` or `quarkus.tls.key-store.pem.<name>.password` property to the "
"password that decrypts the private key."
msgstr ""

msgid "PKCS12 keystores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"PKCS12 keystores are single files that contain the certificate and the "
"private key."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a PKCS12 keystore:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`.p12` files are password-protected, so you need to provide the password to "
"open the keystore."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"These files can contain multiple certificates and private keys.\n"
"If this is the case, take either of the following actions:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Provide and configure the alias of the certificate and the private key you "
"want to use:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Alternatively, use SNI to select the appropriate certificate and private key."
"\n"
"Note that all keys must use the same password."
msgstr ""

msgid "JKS keystores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"JKS keystores are single files that contain the server or client certificate "
"and its private key, used to authenticate and establish secure TLS/SSL "
"connections."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"JKS is an old but still widely used Java-specific format.\n"
"However, to work with this format, you must use specific, and nowadays also "
"deprecated, Java tooling.\n"
"Thus, its use with your {project-name} application is not recommended."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Additionally, OpenShift cert-manager or Let's Encrypt does not typically "
"provide JKS and remains PEM-only."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a JKS keystore:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`.jks` files are password-protected, so you need to provide the password to "
"open the keystore.\n"
"Also, they can include multiple certificates and private keys.\n"
"If this is the case:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Provided keystores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need more control over the keystore used in a TLS configuration, you "
"can provide a CDI bean implementing the `io.quarkus.tls.KeyStoreProvider` "
"interface."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus calls `KeyStoreProvider::getKeyStore` when the TLS configuration is "
"<<startup-checks,initially created>> and any time the configuration is "
"<<reloading-certificates,reloaded>>.\n"
"The resulting keystore and options are then made available via "
"`TlsConfiguration::getKeyStore` and `TlsConfiguration::getKeyStoreOptions`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The CDI bean implementing the `KeyStoreProvider` interface can be "
"`@ApplicationScoped`, `@Singleton`, or `@Dependent`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use the `@Identifier` qualifier to indicate the named TLS configuration that "
"provides the keystore options.\n"
"Omit the qualifier to indicate the default TLS configuration.\n"
"You can also use `@Default` explicitly to indicate the default TLS "
"configuration.\n"
"For more information, see <<referencing-a-tls-configuration>>."
msgstr ""

msgid "You can inject other CDI beans to access keystore material at runtime."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other keystore types"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need to use a keystore type other than PEM, PKCS12, or JKS (for "
"example, Bouncy Castle FIPS `BCFKS`), use the `other` configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The `type` property is required and corresponds to the type passed to `java."
"security.KeyStore.getInstance(type)`.\n"
"The optional `provider` property specifies the Java security provider name.\n"
"All other properties (`path`, `password`, `alias`, `alias-password`) work "
"identically to the JKS and PKCS12 configurations.\n"
"The `other` option is mutually exclusive with `pem`, `p12`, `jks`, and "
"`KeyStoreProvider` beans."
msgstr ""

msgid "Custom keystore factory"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"For keystore types that require custom loading logic beyond `KeyStore."
"getInstance()`, implement a `KeyStoreFactory` CDI bean annotated with "
"`@Identifier` matching the `type` value:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The factory receives the full `OtherKeyStoreConfiguration`, including "
"arbitrary parameters set via `quarkus.tls.key-store.other.params.<key>="
"<value>`.\n"
"If no factory bean is found for the given type, the standard `KeyStore."
"getInstance(type, provider)` loading is used as a fallback."
msgstr ""

msgid "PKCS#11 example"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"PKCS#11 keystores are provider-managed (backed by a hardware security module "
"or software token) and cannot be loaded from a file.\n"
"Use a `KeyStoreFactory` to integrate a PKCS#11 keystore with the TLS "
"registry:"
msgstr ""

msgid "The `@Identifier` value must match the `type` in the configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Arbitrary parameters from `quarkus.tls.key-store.other.params.*` are "
"available via `config.params()`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"PKCS#11 keystores are loaded with `null` — the provider manages the "
"underlying storage."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use `KeyStoreOptionsBase` to let Vert.x create a `KeyManagerFactory` from "
"the provider-backed keystore."
msgstr ""

msgid "SNI"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Server Name Indication (SNI) is a TLS extension that makes it possible for a "
"client to specify the hostname to which it attempts to connect during the "
"TLS handshake.\n"
"SNI enables a server to present multiple TLS certificates for different "
"domains on a single IP address, facilitating secure communication in virtual "
"hosting scenarios."
msgstr ""

msgid "To enable SNI:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"With SNI enabled, the client indicates the server name during the TLS "
"handshake, which allows the server to select the appropriate certificate:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When configuring the keystore with PEM files, multiple certificate (CRT) and "
"key files must be provided.\n"
"CRT is a common file extension for X.509 certificate files, typically in PEM "
"(Privacy-Enhanced Mail) format.\n"
"These files contain the public certificate."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When configuring the keystore with a JKS or P12 file, the server selects the "
"appropriate certificate based on the SNI hostname provided by the client "
"during the TLS handshake.\n"
"The server matches the SNI hostname with the common name (CN) or subject "
"alternative names (SAN) configured in the certificates stored in the "
"keystore.\n"
"All keystore and alias passwords must be identical."
msgstr ""

msgid "Credential providers"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can use a credential provider instead of specifying the keystore and "
"alias passwords in the configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A credential provider offers a way to retrieve the keystore and alias "
"password.\n"
"Quarkus uses the credential provider only when the configuration does not "
"set the keystore password or alias password."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a credential provider:"
msgstr ""

msgid "Trust stores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Trust stores are used to store the certificates of the trusted parties.\n"
"In regular TLS, the client uses a truststore to authenticate the server.\n"
"With mutual TLS (mTLS), both the server and the client use truststores to "
"authenticate each other."
msgstr ""

msgid "PEM truststores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"PEM truststores are composed of a list of `.crt` or `.pem` files.\n"
"Each of them contains a certificate."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a PEM truststore:"
msgstr ""

msgid "PKCS12 truststores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"PKCS12 truststores are single-file containers for certificates.\n"
"You can use the alias to select the appropriate certificate when multiple "
"certificates are included."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a PKCS12 truststore:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`.p12` files are password-protected, so you need to provide the password to "
"open the truststore.\n"
"However, unlike keystores, the alias does not require a password because it "
"contains a public certificate, not a private key."
msgstr ""

msgid "JKS truststores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"JKS truststores are single files that contain multiple certificates.\n"
"You can use the alias to select the appropriate certificate when multiple "
"certificates are present.\n"
"However, avoid using the JKS format, because it is less secure than PKCS12."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure a JKS truststore:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`.jks` files are password-protected, so you need to provide the password to "
"open the truststore.\n"
"However, unlike keystores, the alias does not require a password because it "
"contains a public certificate, not a private key."
msgstr ""

msgid "Provided truststores"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need more control over the truststore used in a TLS configuration, "
"you can provide a CDI bean implementing the `io.quarkus.tls."
"TrustStoreProvider` interface. Quarkus calls `TrustStoreProvider::"
"getTrustStore` when the TLS configuration is <<startup-checks,initially "
"created>> and any time the configuration is <<reloading-"
"certificates,reloaded>>. The resulting truststore and options are then made "
"available via `TlsConfiguration::getTrustStore` and `TlsConfiguration::"
"getTrustStoreOptions`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The CDI bean that implements the `TrustStoreProvider` interface can be "
"`@ApplicationScoped`, `@Singleton`, or `@Dependent`."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Use the `@Identifier` qualifier to indicate the named TLS configuration that "
"provides the truststore options.\n"
"Omit the qualifier to indicate the default TLS configuration.\n"
"You can also use `@Default` explicitly to indicate the default TLS "
"configuration.\n"
"For more information, see <<referencing-a-tls-configuration>>."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can inject other CDI beans to access truststore material at runtime."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other truststore types"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If you need to use a truststore type other than PEM, PKCS12, or JKS, use the "
"`other` configuration:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This works the same as the <<Other keystore types>> configuration described "
"above.\n"
"For custom loading logic, implement a `TrustStoreFactory` CDI bean annotated "
"with `@Identifier` matching the `type` value."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Instead of passing the truststore password in the configuration, you can use "
"a credential provider.\n"
"A credential provider allows you to retrieve passwords and other credentials."
"\n"
"Note that the credential provider is used only if the password is not set in "
"the configuration."
msgstr ""

msgid "Other properties"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"While keystores and truststores are the most important properties, there are "
"other properties you can use to configure TLS."
msgstr ""

msgid "Cipher suites"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Cipher suites are a list of ciphers that you can use during the TLS "
"handshake.\n"
"You can configure an ordered list of enabled cipher suites.\n"
"If not configured, a reasonable default is selected from the built-in "
"ciphers.\n"
"However, when specified, your configuration precedes the default suite "
"defined by the SSL engine in use."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure the cipher suites:"
msgstr ""

msgid "TLS protocol versions"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The TLS protocol versions are the list of protocols that can be used during "
"the TLS handshake.\n"
"Enabled TLS protocol versions are specified as an ordered list separated by "
"commas.\n"
"The relevant configuration property is `quarkus.tls.protocols` or `quarkus."
"tls.<name>.protocols` for named TLS configurations.\n"
"For improved security, it defaults to `TLSv1.3` unless configured otherwise."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The available options are `TLSv1`, `TLSv1.1`, `TLSv1.2`, and `TLSv1.3`."
msgstr ""

msgid "For example, to enable both `TLSv1.3` and `TLSv1.2`:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"If only certain services require `TLSv1.2`,\n"
"it is recommended to configure a dedicated, named TLS configuration for "
"those services\n"
"and keep the default configuration for services that support `TLSv1.3`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Handshake timeout"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When a TLS connection is established, the handshake phase is the first step.\n"
"During this phase, the client and server exchange information to establish "
"the connection, which typically includes the cipher suite, the TLS protocol "
"version, and the certification validation."
msgstr ""

msgid "To configure the timeout for the handshake phase:"
msgstr ""

msgid "ALPN"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Application-Layer Protocol Negotiation (ALPN) is a TLS extension that allows "
"the client and server to negotiate which protocol they will use for "
"communication during the TLS handshake.\n"
"ALPN enables more efficient communication by allowing the client to indicate "
"its preferred application protocol to the server before establishing the TLS "
"connection."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"This helps in scenarios such as HTTP/2, where multiple protocols can be "
"available, enabling faster protocol selection."
msgstr ""

msgid "ALPN is enabled by default."
msgstr ""

msgid "To disable it:"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Disabling ALPN is not recommended for non-experts, as it can lead to "
"performance degradation, protocol negotiation issues, and unexpected "
"behavior, particularly with protocols like HTTP/2.\n"
"However, disabling ALPN can be useful for diagnosing native inconsistencies "
"or testing performance in specific edge cases where protocol negotiation "
"causes conflicts."
msgstr ""

msgid "Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC)"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) protects TLS connections against attacks "
"from quantum computers.\n"
"Quarkus exposes two properties to control post-quantum key exchange, both of "
"which apply to the HTTP server, HTTP client, REST client, and mailer."
msgstr ""

msgid "Key exchange groups"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The key exchange groups (also called _supported groups_) are the elliptic-"
"curve or post-quantum algorithms the TLS engine advertises during the "
"handshake.\n"
"When not set, the engine uses its built-in defaults."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"To advertise the hybrid post-quantum group `X25519MLKEM768` (combining "
"X25519 with ML-KEM-768):"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"You can specify multiple groups as a comma-separated list.\n"
"Groups are tried in the order listed; the first one the peer also supports "
"is used."
msgstr ""

msgid "PQC enforcement policy"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The enforcement policy controls how strictly the server requires post-"
"quantum key exchange from clients."
msgstr ""

msgid "The available values are:"
msgstr ""

msgid "`relaxed`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"No enforcement.\n"
"Standard TLS negotiation applies, and no post-quantum groups are required.\n"
"This is the default."
msgstr ""

msgid "`client-negotiated`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The server advertises post-quantum groups and prefers them, but accepts "
"connections from clients that do not support them, falling back to classical "
"key exchange.\n"
"Use this when you want post-quantum key exchange where available without "
"breaking existing clients."
msgstr ""

msgid "`strict`"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The server only accepts connections that use a post-quantum key exchange "
"group.\n"
"Clients that do not negotiate a post-quantum group are rejected.\n"
"Use this when all clients are known to support post-quantum cryptography."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`pqc-enforcement-policy` does more than enforce post-quantum key exchange: "
"it controls which SSL engine Vert.x selects.\n"
"Setting `pqc-enforcement-policy` to `client-negotiated` or `strict` "
"activates the native OpenSSL engine (requires OpenSSL 3.5+), which "
"negotiates post-quantum key exchange groups.\n"
"With the default `relaxed` policy, Vert.x uses the JDK SSL engine, which "
"ignores any post-quantum key-exchange groups before JDK 27 — meaning that "
"configuring post-quantum groups without also setting the enforcement policy "
"results in no post-quantum key exchange at all.\n"
"On JDK 27+, the JDK natively supports `X25519MLKEM768` and post-quantum key "
"exchange works without changing the enforcement policy."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Quarkus logs a warning at startup if you configure a post-quantum group with "
"`relaxed` policy to help catch this misconfiguration."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"When using `client-negotiated` or `strict`, `quarkus.tls.key-exchange-"
"groups` must include at least one post-quantum-capable group such as "
"`X25519MLKEM768`.\n"
"Vert.x validates this at startup and throws an exception if no compatible "
"engine or group is configured."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"`strict` enforcement will reject any client that does not support the "
"configured post-quantum group, including standard browsers and TLS clients "
"that have not yet adopted post-quantum extensions.\n"
"Ensure all clients are upgraded before enabling this mode."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Policy enforcement (`strict` and `client-negotiated`) is not supported when "
"using the JDK SSL engine.\n"
"If you are running on JDK 27+, or on JDK 25+ once the post-quantum backport "
"ships (expected October 2026), you can still enable post-quantum key "
"exchange by setting `quarkus.tls.key-exchange-groups` — the negotiation will "
"work correctly without policy enforcement.\n"
"For full enforcement, OpenSSL 3.5+ via the native SSL engine is required."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Post-quantum enforcement (`client-negotiated` and `strict`) relies on a "
"native OpenSSL 3.5+ library.\n"
"Quarkus bundles this library only for **Linux x86_64** and **Linux aarch64**."
"\n"
"In addition, `libapr-1` must be installed on the host OS (see link:https://"
"netty.io/wiki/forked-tomcat-native.html[netty-tcnative prerequisites]).\n"
"Other operating systems and architectures (macOS, Windows, Alpine Linux, "
"Linux on RISC-V, etc.) are *not supported*: attempting to use `client-"
"negotiated` or `strict` on an unsupported platform will fail at runtime "
